\details{
A score-driven level, the first level of \code{\link[modelterms7:regime]{modelterms7::regime()}} and each
level of a joint filter add a constant to their equation's predictor.
Where an intercept is present the level is held (\code{\link[=statmod_held_levels]{statmod_held_levels()}})
and the intercept is integrated as an unpenalized coefficient. Where the
level is free, it plays the intercept's part, and the criterion integrates
it in the same way: as the constant that it adds to the predictor, a
column of ones in its equation. For \code{\link[modelterms7:gas]{modelterms7::gas()}} that constant is
the stationary level \eqn{\omega / (1 - \sum_j \xi_j)}, so
\code{y ~ x + random(~ 1 | g) + gas()} and \code{y ~ 0 + x + random(~ 1 | g) + gas()} give the same criterion at every hyperparameter.

The rule applies to \code{\link[=reml]{reml()}} on the route over the coefficients, where no
penalty covers the term's own parameters; where one does,
\code{\link[=statmod_marginal_full]{statmod_marginal_full()}} already integrates every free parameter of the
term. \code{\link[=ml]{ml()}} integrates the penalized directions only and is unchanged. A
developed level contributes its constant coordinate, the one that
\code{\link[modelterms7:term_level_param]{modelterms7::term_level_param()}} names. A model carrying a Jeffreys
penalty is left on its own route, whose curvature is factored in two
parts.
